@node	0.3, 0.4, Chapter 0, Chapter 0
@section Bindings, functions, and modules

@noindent
@code{let} inside an expression names a value for the rest of that
expression:

@example
# let size = 2 in 5 + 3 + size;;
- : int = 10
@end example

@noindent
At the top level, the @code{in} is dropped, and the binding stays in scope
for whatever comes after it:

@example
# let x = 5;;
val x : int = 5
# let x = x + 1;;
val x : int = 6
# x;;
- : int = 6
@end example

@noindent
The second @code{let x} does not mutate the first: it @newterm{shadows} it,
introducing a new binding that happens to reuse the name. The old @code{x}
still exists, invisibly, to anyone holding a closure built before the
shadowing binding took effect; nothing about @code{x} itself changed. This
matters once section 0.7 covers closures: shadowing a name in an outer scope
never reaches back to change what an already-built closure sees.

@subsubheading Functions

@code{let f @var{params} = @var{body}} defines a function:

@example
# let square x = x * x;;
val square : int -> int = <fun>
# let sum_of_squares x y = square x + square y;;
val sum_of_squares : int -> int -> int = <fun>
# sum_of_squares 3 4;;
- : int = 25
@end example

@noindent
The inferred type @code{int -> int -> int} reads as ``a function from
@code{int} to a function from @code{int} to @code{int}'': every OCaml
function of more than one argument is really a chain of one-argument
functions, a fact section 0.7 puts to direct use. A function body can bind
its own local names before producing a result:

@example
# let f x =
    let a = x + 1 in
    a + square a;;
val f : int -> int = <fun>
# f 3;;
- : int = 20
@end example

@noindent
Recursion needs one extra word: @code{let rec}, not plain @code{let}. Without
@code{rec}, the name being defined is not yet in scope inside its own body,
so a recursive call inside a plain @code{let} would refer to some outer
binding of the same name (or fail to compile, if there is none) rather than
recurse:

@example
# let rec factorial n = if n = 0 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1);;
val factorial : int -> int = <fun>
# factorial 6;;
- : int = 720
@end example

@subsubheading Partial application

Every OCaml function takes its arguments one at a time. Supplying fewer than
all of them returns a new function waiting for the rest. No special syntax, just an application that stops early:

@example
# let inc = ( + ) 1;;
val inc : int -> int = <fun>
# inc 41;;
- : int = 42
# let double = ( * ) 2;;
val double : int -> int = <fun>
# double 8;;
- : int = 16
@end example

@noindent
@code{( + )} is the addition operator written as an ordinary value (parens
around an infix operator turn it into a prefix one), so @code{( + ) 1} is
addition with its first argument already fixed at @code{1}: the successor
function. This is the same mechanism as any partial application; it works
because a function of two arguments genuinely is a function returning a
function, not two-argument application syntax bolted onto something else.

@subsubheading Labeled arguments

A parameter name prefixed with @code{~} becomes a label the caller writes at
the call site, useful when a function's arguments could otherwise be
confused for each other:

@example
# let pad_to ~width s =
  String.make (max 0 (width - String.length s)) '.' ^ s;;
val pad_to : width:int -> string -> string = <fun>
# pad_to ~width:8 "sicp";;
- : string = "....sicp"
@end example

@noindent
This book uses labeled arguments sparingly, only where a call site would
otherwise read as an ambiguous pair of numbers or strings; most functions in
this edition take plain positional arguments, relying on clear parameter
names rather than call-site labels.

@subsubheading Modules and dotted names

A name from another module is reached by a dot, the way @code{List.length}
reaches the @code{length} function of the standard library's @code{List}
module:

@example
# List.length [ 1; 2; 3 ];;
- : int = 3
@end example

@noindent
Every module in this book's own code is split into two files: an
implementation, @file{@var{name}.ml}, and an interface,
@file{@var{name}.mli}, that states exactly which of the implementation's
definitions are visible outside it, and at what type. @file{sec_0_3.mli}
alongside this section's @file{sec_0_3.ml} is a first example: it lists
@code{square}, @code{sum_of_squares}, @code{f}, @code{factorial},
@code{inc}, @code{double}, and @code{pad_to}, each with a one-line comment in
the interface describing what it computes. An @file{.mli} is where a
module's documentation lives: every module in this book carries one.
